# `!in ~` is `!in` then `~`: the `~` must follow directly.
mode: syntax
---- query
from t | where x !in ~ ('a')
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..29
  PIPELINE@0..28
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    NAME_REF@5..6
      IDENT@5..6 "t"
    WHITESPACE@6..7 " "
    PIPE@7..8 "|"
    WHITESPACE@8..9 " "
    WHERE_OP@9..28
      IDENT@9..14 "where"
      WHITESPACE@14..15 " "
      IN_EXPR@15..28
        NAME_REF@15..16
          IDENT@15..16 "x"
        WHITESPACE@16..17 " "
        NEGATED_OP@17..20 "!in"
        WHITESPACE@20..21 " "
        TILDE@21..22 "~"
        WHITESPACE@22..23 " "
        ARG_LIST@23..28
          L_PAREN@23..24 "("
          LITERAL@24..27
            STRING@24..27 "'a'"
          R_PAREN@27..28 ")"
  WHITESPACE@28..29 "\n"

error[DS0129]: `!in~` is written without a space
  --> query:1:18
  |
1 | from t | where x !in ~ ('a')
  |                  ^^^^^ read as `!in` followed by `~`
  |
  = help: write `!in~` to match the list ignoring case

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    from t | where x !in~ ('a')
which parses without errors
